
Moving to use the VALOR framework rather than a standalone analysis package entailed a number of changes, which will mean the macros will generally need to be updated.

A (probably incomplete) list of changes that need to be made. Any questions, direct to Steve Dennis.

Default mixing param choice is now sinsq(theta23), not sinsq(2theta23) (fmwk r3999)

namespace t2kana => valor (find and replace)

FitParameterList::NamedParameterList -> util::NamedParameterList
FitInputs::Chisquare => FitInputs::Statistic
FitInputs::BestFitChisquare => FitParamList::GetBestFitStatistic

Skobs/Skexp's GetSpectrum (histogram) methods now allocate a new histogram, which should be deleted or it'll leak memory over time (if you call skobs->GetCurrErecoHist()->Integral() for example). Not really important for short macros.

***IMPORTANT*** After setting analysis options in Opt() like dmsq scheme and mixing param choice, you should  then call Opt::Instance()->SetDefaultParameterValues()

Opt::gOscAnaTypeChoice now lives in T2KOpt, but not many macros need this.

Since PRODUCTIONMODE is always on now, macros should be calling SuperkMC->Get2DHist(...) instead of Template(...) (returns by value, not pointer)

As of fmwk revision r3983, FitterMinuit now correctly fills the MigradErrors and HesseErrors function in FitParamList. You should explicitly ask for Hesse errors. On which note, you *need* to explicitly ask for Hesse fit with fit option "minuit+hesse"

I'm trying to make the code support python macros wherever possible. In general, you get this for free with ROOT, but python can't (easily) handle functions overloaded with different enums, so in this case (like AsString) it's probably good practice to ensure there is a uniquely named version that can be called from python.

